WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.801
-You know our first guest
tonight from his work

00:00:02.801 --> 00:00:04.070
on "Saturday Night Live,"

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:06.105
where he serves as one
of the head writers

00:00:06.105 --> 00:00:07.707
as well as co-anchor
of Weekend Update.

00:00:07.707 --> 00:00:11.177
Season 48 of SNL premieres
Saturday, October 1st

00:00:11.177 --> 00:00:13.979
on NBC and Peacock
with host Miles Teller

00:00:13.979 --> 00:00:15.548
and musical guest
Kendrick Lamar.

00:00:15.548 --> 00:00:17.083
Please welcome back
to the show

00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:19.419
our very good friend
Colin Jost, everybody.

00:00:19.419 --> 00:00:21.421
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:21.421 --> 00:00:30.463 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:30.463 --> 00:00:39.505 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:39.505 --> 00:00:40.840
Yeah!
[ Cheers and applause continue ]

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:41.841
I mean...

00:00:41.841 --> 00:00:42.841
-Ah!

00:00:42.841 --> 00:00:44.176
-Very nice to see you.

00:00:44.176 --> 00:00:45.645
-Hi. Thank you.

00:00:45.645 --> 00:00:47.413
That's very nice.
-And look, we're the real --

00:00:47.413 --> 00:00:49.248
I mean, no offense to Tom --

00:00:49.248 --> 00:00:51.317
We're the real Blue Man Group.
I mean, look at us.

00:00:51.317 --> 00:00:52.985
[ Laughter ]

00:00:52.985 --> 00:00:54.053
-That's right. It's great.

00:00:54.053 --> 00:00:55.555
It's gonna be great
branding for us.

00:00:55.555 --> 00:00:57.357
-I'm so happy to have you
back in the building.

00:00:57.357 --> 00:00:58.925
You're gone --
you were gone all summer.

00:00:58.925 --> 00:01:01.927
Did you have a nice summer?
Was it laid-back?

00:01:01.927 --> 00:01:03.529
-I know.
I feel like we get to --

00:01:03.529 --> 00:01:05.531
We have to like book appearances
to get to hang out together.

00:01:05.531 --> 00:01:06.799
-We're very old friends

00:01:06.799 --> 00:01:08.134
and we only see each other
on talk show appearances.

00:01:08.134 --> 00:01:09.602
-I know, I know.
It's wonderful.

00:01:09.602 --> 00:01:10.703
Yeah, I had a great summer.

00:01:10.703 --> 00:01:12.539
Very...
We had a lot of family time.

00:01:12.539 --> 00:01:14.039
We got to relax.

00:01:14.039 --> 00:01:17.077
Like, I actually took some time
off to be with our baby, too.

00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:19.145
-How old is --
how old is Cosmo now?

00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:21.681
He -- He turned one.

00:01:21.681 --> 00:01:23.516
He's somewhere over one. Um...

00:01:23.516 --> 00:01:25.017
[ Laughter ]
I don't -- I don't c--

00:01:25.017 --> 00:01:27.620
Like, you know, I don't ever
need to know beyond that.

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:29.922
Yeah, like when people are like,
"Oh, it's 15 months."

00:01:29.922 --> 00:01:32.258
I'm like, that doesn't mean
anything to me.

00:01:32.258 --> 00:01:33.259
Does not change --

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:35.060
Just like,
tell me when he's one.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:36.996
Because then, before that,
he's zero, you know?

00:01:36.996 --> 00:01:40.133
Then, and then I don't really
need to know till he's like --

00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:41.934
till he's like five.
-Five? Okay.

00:01:41.934 --> 00:01:43.269
I feel like one --
-So, you want to know one.

00:01:43.269 --> 00:01:44.404
-Five...

00:01:44.404 --> 00:01:46.906
And then I guess 25?
[ Laughter ]

00:01:46.906 --> 00:01:49.509
Like, in case I need him
to, like, you know,

00:01:49.509 --> 00:01:50.843
rent a car to take me somewhere.

00:01:50.843 --> 00:01:52.145
-Oh, that's helpful.
-Yeah.

00:01:52.145 --> 00:01:54.480
-So, basically, one to five,

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.516
that's one chunk for you,
and then you don't care again

00:01:56.516 --> 00:01:58.151
until they can help you
rent a car.

00:01:58.151 --> 00:01:59.718
-Yeah.
Yeah, like you're --

00:01:59.718 --> 00:02:01.353
you have three kids,
so they're...

00:02:01.353 --> 00:02:04.690
to me, they're like one...

00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:05.958
-I have one -- Yeah.
So, I have one --

00:02:05.958 --> 00:02:08.260
-They're zero...
-I have two ones and a five.

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:09.895
-Yeah, two ones and a five.
-Yeah.

00:02:09.895 --> 00:02:11.764
-So, you'll tell me when
the other ones are five, right?

00:02:11.764 --> 00:02:13.132
-Yeah, exactly.
-We're fine.

00:02:13.132 --> 00:02:16.235
-I mean, you don't mind
if I call you right away?

00:02:16.235 --> 00:02:17.937
-I mean...alright.

00:02:17.937 --> 00:02:19.439
-Do you...

00:02:19.439 --> 00:02:23.209
So, again, I consider you
kind of a laid-back guy,

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:25.044
but being a parent,
totally different.

00:02:25.044 --> 00:02:27.546
Like, all of a sudden,
I feel you experience fear

00:02:27.546 --> 00:02:28.881
in a way you never had before.

00:02:28.881 --> 00:02:31.450
Do you have any fears
about being a parent?

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:33.219
-Lots of fears about
being a parent, yes.

00:02:33.219 --> 00:02:34.520
I mean, the hard --

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:38.024
I think when you have a decent
imagination, it's worse.

00:02:38.024 --> 00:02:40.893
because you can --
you don't have rational fears.

00:02:40.893 --> 00:02:42.962
You have creative fears.
-Yeah.

00:02:42.962 --> 00:02:45.631
-You know, like we actually
are not doing anything

00:02:45.631 --> 00:02:48.968
to prevent the rational
problems that could happen.

00:02:48.968 --> 00:02:51.804
Like, we're not -- we've done
no baby-proofing.

00:02:51.804 --> 00:02:54.540
Every electrical outlet
is wide open.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.509
And a lot of them
are the old ones,

00:02:56.509 --> 00:02:57.910
like where they have,
like, you know,

00:02:57.910 --> 00:02:59.846
baby-finger-sized holes.

00:02:59.846 --> 00:03:01.514
-Yeah.
-And like, if you look in,

00:03:01.514 --> 00:03:03.249
you see a little spark
that could,

00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:05.184
like, catch a baby's gaze,
you know?

00:03:05.184 --> 00:03:08.354
-Yeah.
-And we have, like, every table

00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:12.592
has, like, glass objects --
every coffee table.

00:03:12.592 --> 00:03:17.930
One has just like a ceramic dish
with small glass balls in it.

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:20.566
-You brought a photo of this.
So, first of all, like...

00:03:20.566 --> 00:03:23.236
Even if you don't have a baby,

00:03:23.236 --> 00:03:25.471
this is a [bleep] thing to have.

00:03:25.471 --> 00:03:27.806
[ Laughter ]

00:03:27.806 --> 00:03:29.308
-[ Laughing ] I mean, I --
-What is this?

00:03:29.308 --> 00:03:30.776
-I mean, I...

00:03:30.776 --> 00:03:33.446
This is kind of shocking --
I wasn't the one who bought it.

00:03:33.446 --> 00:03:35.448
But I don't...

00:03:35.448 --> 00:03:37.950
No offense to
the wonderful artist,

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:40.752
but this is for baby choking.
This is just...

00:03:40.752 --> 00:03:42.588
There's no other...
-No, what baby -- first of all,

00:03:42.588 --> 00:03:45.190
like, even if you're like. "Oh,
don't worry, we'll zip it up,"

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:48.194
like, any baby is gonna be like,
"I'm gonna unzip that!"

00:03:48.194 --> 00:03:49.529
-And the great thing about this,

00:03:49.529 --> 00:03:50.562
it doesn't zip up.

00:03:50.562 --> 00:03:51.564
It's fake.

00:03:51.564 --> 00:03:54.233
It's a fake ceramic zipper.

00:03:54.233 --> 00:03:56.368
And I honestly think that
when our baby goes

00:03:56.368 --> 00:03:58.871
and picks them up,
it's almost like if you see

00:03:58.871 --> 00:04:02.008
someone dressed in a suit
in a bad neighborhood.

00:04:02.008 --> 00:04:04.276
Where, like, "We should rob him,
but it seems like a trap."

00:04:04.276 --> 00:04:06.112
It's too obvious.
It's got to be...

00:04:06.112 --> 00:04:07.480
I think he looks at it,
but he's like,

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:09.548
"This is too obvious
a choking hazard. I can't."

00:04:09.548 --> 00:04:11.851
-Yeah, right.
-"I'm not gonna fall for this."

00:04:11.851 --> 00:04:13.653
-Yeah, it looks like --

00:04:13.653 --> 00:04:16.922
like a sandwich underneath a box
that has, like, a stick.

00:04:16.922 --> 00:04:19.291
-A stick, where you're like,
"There's something else."

00:04:19.291 --> 00:04:21.394
-I should note that I didn't
realize this was ceramic.

00:04:21.394 --> 00:04:23.229
I have more appreciation
for this now...

00:04:23.229 --> 00:04:24.831
-Really?
-...then when I first said.

00:04:24.831 --> 00:04:26.999
I mean, I bet if you told me
what it cost,

00:04:26.999 --> 00:04:28.668
I might go back to [bleep]

00:04:28.668 --> 00:04:30.837
[ Laughter ]

00:04:30.837 --> 00:04:33.105
But I am impressed
with the actual craftsmanship.

00:04:33.105 --> 00:04:35.575
-Yeah, yeah, no, it's --
the craftsmanship's great.

00:04:35.575 --> 00:04:38.244
No, the artist is gonna
love the plug now.

00:04:38.244 --> 00:04:40.046
[ Laughter ]

00:04:43.215 --> 00:04:44.850
Well, so, we don't have...

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:46.753
So, we don't really do the --
but I have a lot.

00:04:46.753 --> 00:04:49.288
My fear, like my number one fear

00:04:49.288 --> 00:04:51.624
for what could happen to him --

00:04:51.624 --> 00:04:53.126
eagles.

00:04:53.126 --> 00:04:54.293
[ Laughter ]

00:04:54.293 --> 00:04:55.795
'Cause bald eagles --

00:04:55.795 --> 00:04:56.862
I don't know
if you know this --

00:04:56.862 --> 00:04:58.197
-Unh-unh.
-Bald eagles are back.

00:04:58.197 --> 00:04:59.531
-They are?
-In a big way.

00:04:59.531 --> 00:05:01.367
And everyone's like,
"Oh, yay, they're back!"

00:05:01.367 --> 00:05:02.801
-Yeah.
-Till you got a baby.

00:05:02.801 --> 00:05:04.503
You know, it's the same way
people are like,

00:05:04.503 --> 00:05:06.572
"Oh, the shark population's
come back!"

00:05:06.572 --> 00:05:08.173
Till you look,
like every other day,

00:05:08.173 --> 00:05:10.576
a guy in Long Island's
getting torn to pieces.

00:05:10.576 --> 00:05:12.377
You know what I mean?
Not every other day, but...

00:05:12.377 --> 00:05:14.380
But I am -- I mean, people are
like, "Oh, it's great" --

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:15.881
-I will buy that people
are excited

00:05:15.881 --> 00:05:17.282
that bald eagles are back.

00:05:17.282 --> 00:05:19.819
I've not heard a single person
be like, "Hooray, sharks!"

00:05:19.819 --> 00:05:21.320
[ Laughter ]

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:22.688
-My grandmother
did tell me, like,

00:05:22.688 --> 00:05:24.891
"Did you hear sharks -- there's
now sharks in the ocean?"

00:05:24.891 --> 00:05:26.225
I was like, "Now?"

00:05:26.225 --> 00:05:28.327
[ Laughter ]
But they...

00:05:28.327 --> 00:05:30.529
No, but it is great
that the shark population

00:05:30.529 --> 00:05:32.665
has quadrupled
and moved closer to shore.

00:05:32.665 --> 00:05:34.266
That's really great.
I think everyone loves that.

00:05:34.266 --> 00:05:37.403
-That's great.
-Eagles, to me, they're around.

00:05:37.403 --> 00:05:38.871
-Uh-huh.
-And they're...

00:05:38.871 --> 00:05:40.706
I mean, I haven't done
the research on this,

00:05:40.706 --> 00:05:42.374
but I believe they're

00:05:42.374 --> 00:05:46.145
the largest birds of prey
in America.

00:05:46.145 --> 00:05:48.447
I would maybe even say
in the Americas.

00:05:48.447 --> 00:05:50.282
I mean...
-Yeah. I'm gonna just --

00:05:50.282 --> 00:05:52.051
I'm gonna just put money
that you're wrong.

00:05:52.051 --> 00:05:54.152
-Yeah.
-But you keep going.

00:05:54.152 --> 00:05:55.387
-Well, I mean, America --

00:05:55.387 --> 00:05:56.823
Costa Rica,
obviously, there's --

00:05:56.823 --> 00:05:58.957
there's islands off the coast
where there's pterodactyls.

00:05:58.957 --> 00:06:00.759
I mean, that doesn't --
I'm not --

00:06:00.759 --> 00:06:04.130
I'm not counting --
I'm not counting Isla --

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:06.131
-Anywhere...Nothing, like...

00:06:06.131 --> 00:06:08.000
Non-theme-park birds.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:09.335
-Birds. Yeah.

00:06:09.335 --> 00:06:11.036
Then I think
that they're the largest.

00:06:11.036 --> 00:06:13.840
-Yeah.
-And you know, there's more now,

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:16.408
so there's competition for food.

00:06:16.408 --> 00:06:18.778
You know,
Thanksgiving's coming up,

00:06:18.778 --> 00:06:20.946
and so they're probably like,
"Oh, I could grab that baby,

00:06:20.946 --> 00:06:24.016
feed these -- feed these
baby eagles for weeks!"

00:06:24.016 --> 00:06:26.185
I don't know what their
Thanksgiving traditions are.

00:06:26.185 --> 00:06:27.953
-Yeah.
-We do a turkey.

00:06:27.953 --> 00:06:30.322
But bald eagles might do babies.
-Yeah.

00:06:30.322 --> 00:06:31.490
-And maybe one of them's like,

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:32.825
"Oh, maybe this year
we'll just do ham."

00:06:32.825 --> 00:06:34.494
But it's like,
"No, we gotta do a baby.

00:06:34.494 --> 00:06:35.828
It's Thanksgiving!"
You know what I mean?

00:06:35.828 --> 00:06:37.930
-Right. Thanksgiving.
-So I'm terr-- I'm like...

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:40.099
We go for walks and
I'm scanning the horizon.

00:06:40.099 --> 00:06:41.934
-Sure.
You're just on eagle watch.

00:06:41.934 --> 00:06:43.268
-I'm on eagle watch.

00:06:43.268 --> 00:06:45.438
And then we go for a walk

00:06:45.438 --> 00:06:47.639
and she's got Cosmo
in the stroller,

00:06:47.639 --> 00:06:50.809
and Scarlett puts
a little hat on his head

00:06:50.809 --> 00:06:52.578
that has mouse ears.

00:06:52.578 --> 00:06:56.249
[ Laughter ]

00:06:56.249 --> 00:06:57.883
On the hat!
And I'm like,

00:06:57.883 --> 00:06:59.752
"You're dressing our baby
like food!"

00:06:59.752 --> 00:07:01.754
-Yeah.

00:07:01.754 --> 00:07:04.257
-He's full of these glass balls.

00:07:04.257 --> 00:07:06.592
[ Laughter ]

00:07:06.592 --> 00:07:09.061
It's -- It's eagle entrapment.

00:07:09.061 --> 00:07:11.230
And of course, by the way,
bald eagle --

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:12.665
they're bald, right?

00:07:12.665 --> 00:07:14.232
Who are they gonna go after?

00:07:14.232 --> 00:07:16.268
-Oh, right, right, right.
-If they're angry at anyone...

00:07:16.268 --> 00:07:18.104
It's one of the only things
I've got going for me.

00:07:18.104 --> 00:07:19.605
-Really good head of hair.
-These eagles are looking at me

00:07:19.605 --> 00:07:20.639
going...
-"Should take that guy's baby."

00:07:20.639 --> 00:07:21.974
-Yeah, that baby.

00:07:21.974 --> 00:07:26.645
-You should have that artist
make a ceramic Cosmo.

00:07:26.645 --> 00:07:28.280
-And try to feed it...?
-A decoy.

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:29.882
-Try to choke an eagle with it?
-Yeah!

00:07:29.882 --> 00:07:31.550
Like, an eagle comes down
thinking

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:33.485
it's like a nice, soft baby,
and they're just like --

00:07:33.485 --> 00:07:34.820
Bam!
-Doink!

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:38.324
-[ Laughing ] Doink!
He's gonna doink right off it.

00:07:38.324 --> 00:07:39.959
-Hey...
-I'm great with sound effects.

00:07:39.959 --> 00:07:41.494
They never let me do
my own sound effects.

00:07:41.494 --> 00:07:42.962
-They never do.
It's such a bummer.

00:07:42.962 --> 00:07:46.131
Hey, you and...
I want to check in on the...

00:07:46.131 --> 00:07:49.968
You and Pete Davidson bought
an old Staten Island ferry.

00:07:49.968 --> 00:07:51.236
-Yes. That's correct.

00:07:51.236 --> 00:07:53.238
And you know I'm a professional

00:07:53.238 --> 00:07:54.807
'cause I'm wearing
the life jacket on shore.

00:07:54.807 --> 00:07:56.308
-You just can tell.

00:07:56.308 --> 00:07:58.143
[ Laughter ]
This is...

00:07:58.143 --> 00:08:00.446
You know how, like, sometimes
your friends do something

00:08:00.446 --> 00:08:02.114
and you're like,
"Aw, why didn't they ask me?"

00:08:02.114 --> 00:08:03.916
That is not how I feel about it.

00:08:03.916 --> 00:08:05.418
[ Laughter ]

00:08:05.418 --> 00:08:07.920
But do give me an update
on the ferry that you bought

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:09.154
with Pete Davidson.
-Oh, my God.

00:08:09.154 --> 00:08:11.423
Financially, it's going great.

00:08:11.423 --> 00:08:13.092
[ Laughter ]
I mean, it is...

00:08:13.092 --> 00:08:16.062
We are -- we are printing money.

00:08:16.062 --> 00:08:18.430
It's -- It is our own money.
It's ferry bucks.

00:08:18.430 --> 00:08:19.931
But we are printing it,

00:08:19.931 --> 00:08:23.068
desperately hoping that a dock
will take it for payment.

00:08:23.068 --> 00:08:24.703
-So, you don't have a dock yet?

00:08:24.703 --> 00:08:27.139
-It's basically docked.
I mean, it's not gonna

00:08:27.139 --> 00:08:29.908
just drift into the Manhattan
or anything.

00:08:29.908 --> 00:08:31.310
It'll be fine.
-That's great.

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:32.711
-It's tied down.

00:08:32.711 --> 00:08:34.380
-Is it exciting to buy a ferry

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:36.182
right as the sharks come back?

00:08:36.182 --> 00:08:38.117
[ Laughter ]

00:08:38.117 --> 00:08:39.418
We bought...

00:08:39.418 --> 00:08:41.587
This is why idiots should not
be allowed to do things.

00:08:41.587 --> 00:08:42.821
-Yeah.

00:08:42.821 --> 00:08:44.590
-Pete and I bought this boat,
and then, like,

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:47.025
there's so many immediate
decisions you have to make,

00:08:47.025 --> 00:08:48.761
and one of them is like,
well, you have to create

00:08:48.761 --> 00:08:51.197
a financial entity
to deal with this boat.

00:08:51.197 --> 00:08:52.798
And so we're on a call,
and I'm just like,

00:08:52.798 --> 00:08:54.667
"Alright, well,
how about Titanic 2?"

00:08:54.667 --> 00:08:56.334
[ Laughter ]

00:08:56.334 --> 00:08:58.070
You know? And so, everyone
on the call is like,

00:08:58.070 --> 00:08:59.872
"Oh, that's great,
let's do that!"

00:08:59.872 --> 00:09:02.274
So that goes into a contract.
Right?

00:09:02.274 --> 00:09:04.910
And so now suddenly
we're looking for insurance,

00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:08.681
which we have to buy 'cause
it's a giant deathtrap boat.

00:09:08.681 --> 00:09:10.516
So you have to get insurance.

00:09:10.516 --> 00:09:11.917
And then every
insurance company's like,

00:09:11.917 --> 00:09:14.086
"It's called Titanic 2?"

00:09:14.086 --> 00:09:15.655
[ Laughter ]

00:09:18.023 --> 00:09:19.525
And they're like --
same like as you,

00:09:19.525 --> 00:09:20.859
they're like, "Is it docked?"

00:09:20.859 --> 00:09:23.361
All these questions!
You're like...

00:09:23.361 --> 00:09:25.531
-That's one question!

00:09:25.531 --> 00:09:27.599
That's the first question
anyone's...

00:09:27.599 --> 00:09:29.001
I like that you're like,
"Stop grilling me!"

00:09:29.001 --> 00:09:30.803
They're like,
"Where does it live?"

00:09:30.803 --> 00:09:33.039
-It's fine!

00:09:33.039 --> 00:09:36.141
You have to also give it
its own independence.

00:09:36.141 --> 00:09:38.077
-The boat?
-[ Laughing ] Just...

00:09:38.077 --> 00:09:39.245
Next question!

00:09:39.245 --> 00:09:40.445
[ Laughter ]

00:09:40.445 --> 00:09:42.114
-Now you're in the building,
you're back.

00:09:42.114 --> 00:09:43.916
-Yeah, yeah, we're back.
-You know, for the whole year.

00:09:43.916 --> 00:09:46.285
You'll have to come back soon.
We want an update on everything.

00:09:46.285 --> 00:09:47.953
And it's just always
the best to see, buddy.

00:09:47.953 --> 00:09:49.388
I love you so very much.
-Aww.

00:09:49.388 --> 00:09:51.723
-You guys, that's Colin Jost.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:51.723 --> 00:09:54.159
SNL returns live this week
for its season premiere

00:09:54.159 --> 00:09:55.627
with host Miles Teller

00:09:55.627 --> 00:09:56.895
and musical guest
Kendrick Lamar.

00:09:56.895 --> 00:09:57.730
We'll be right back.

